  {"type": "split", "photo": "sys", "h2": "Repair or replace? The rule we use for Brampton furnaces",
   "html": "<p>Most repair-or-replace advice is built on age alone. In Brampton we add a second number: run hours. A furnace carrying a family on two floors plus a tenant in the basement can run twice the hours of one in a quiet retirement bungalow. A 14-year-old furnace in a full house has done the work of a much older one, and its blower bearings, ignitor and heat exchanger show it.</p>"
           "<p>So we ask three questions before recommending anything. How old is it? What failed? And how hard has it been working? A cheap part on a lightly used furnace gets repaired, every time. A blower motor, inducer or control board on a hard-working furnace past 15 years usually tips toward replacement, because the next failure is already queued up behind this one.</p>"
           "<ul class=\"checklist\"><li>Ignitors, flame sensors, capacitors and pressure switches: repair</li><li>Blower, inducer or board on a furnace under 12 years: usually repair</li><li>The same parts on a furnace past 15 years in a full house: compare both prices</li><li>Any crack in the heat exchanger: replace, no exceptions</li></ul>"
           "<p>Replacement in a multi-generational home is also the moment to fix the layout, with a separate system for the suite or a heat pump added for cooling and shoulder-season heat. Our <a href=\"/brampton/\">Brampton HVAC page</a> walks through those options, and the province-wide <a href=\"/furnace/repair-maintenance/\">furnace repair and maintenance</a> page covers tune-ups and error codes for any furnace in Ontario.</p>"},
